Harden the run pipeline: migrations, retries, time limit, lease, safe updates

Database
- schema versioned with PRAGMA user_version; missing migrations are
  applied in one transaction at startup, old databases upgrade in
  place, a newer schema is refused;
- bounded connection pool (8) instead of one connection per thread;
- index on errors(day); old errors and processed rows are pruned;
- LIKE wildcards in search are escaped;
- run lease table so only one process can run the pipeline, and a run
  is closed as interrupted only after its heartbeat goes stale.

Pipeline
- run bookkeeping is in try/finally: the run is always finished
  (failed on an exception), progress reset and the lease released;
- a game that failed is retried the same day, up to
  FAILED_GAME_MAX_ATTEMPTS (2), ahead of new games;
- the run stops taking new games after RUN_MAX_MINUTES (50) and keeps
  the day cursor, so the rest is picked up next run;
- New Releases failing falls back to SEE ALL and stays open; a SEE ALL
  page failing keeps what was already selected;
- empty platforms/reviews from Metacritic never overwrite stored data,
  the drift is logged as a :schema error;
- let's plays are refreshed after 7 days when concluded from a
  transcript and after 24 hours otherwise, instead of on every pass;
- games without a Metacritic video get a YouTube trailer (weekly);
- the backfill rotates by last attempt and moves on when one game
  fails, and also upgrades description-based let's play conclusions;
- the clock is injectable.

Scheduler
- tick() is a pure step driven by an injected clock; the interval
  counts from the last run of any kind, a due slot is not dropped
  while another run is active, exceptions never kill the thread;
- health() reports a dead thread or an overdue slot.

Tests cover resume after an interrupted run, same-day retries and the
cap, a run crossing midnight, slug reuse with a new id, partial data,
a game that blocks the backfill, the time limit, the LLM circuit, the
let's play refresh periods, migrations, the pool and the lease.

